1
00:00:00,422 --> 00:00:03,912
An order issue arrives and the team
needs three things.

2
00:00:04,020 --> 00:00:07,698
Someone must explain the validator, find
the rule in our notes,

3
00:00:07,913 --> 00:00:08,981
and label the issue.

4
00:00:09,639 --> 00:00:14,059
Those tasks can share the same text, yet
the outputs are different.

5
00:00:14,166 --> 00:00:16,314
Start by naming the deliverable.

6
00:00:16,394 --> 00:00:20,534
Once you know what must come back, you
can pick a capability and a way to check

7
00:00:20,588 --> 00:00:21,222
the result.

8
00:00:23,168 --> 00:00:28,806
Give Copilot the return line and ask it
to explain the result for zero and one.

9
00:00:28,886 --> 00:00:32,487
A language model can produce an
explanation in ordinary words.

10
00:00:33,155 --> 00:00:35,759
Check those words against the
expression.

11
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:38,578
Both numbers satisfy this condition.

12
00:00:38,659 --> 00:00:42,794
If the answer says zero is rejected,
you've found a specific sentence to

13
00:00:42,847 --> 00:00:45,747
question and a one-line source to point
to.

14
00:00:48,054 --> 00:00:51,759
Now imagine the rule lives in one of
three training notes.

15
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,739
An embedding model turns the query and
the stored notes into vectors.

16
00:00:56,393 --> 00:01:00,206
The system compares those
representations and ranks possible

17
00:01:00,259 --> 00:01:01,468
matches.

18
00:01:01,548 --> 00:01:04,636
A useful first result is the order
acceptance note.

19
00:01:05,295 --> 00:01:07,094
Open it and read the rule.

20
00:01:07,174 --> 00:01:09,403
Similarity helps you find a source.

21
00:01:09,456 --> 00:01:11,975
The source itself tells you what the
rule says.

22
00:01:13,967 --> 00:01:18,832
For the issue label, our outputs are
defect, feature, or question.

23
00:01:18,886 --> 00:01:22,697
The ticket reports behavior that already
contradicts a documented rule.

24
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:26,052
That gives us a reason to choose defect.

25
00:01:26,132 --> 00:01:30,670
A classifier can propose the label
directly, and a language model can also

26
00:01:30,723 --> 00:01:32,591
choose from a short allowed list.

27
00:01:33,261 --> 00:01:37,100
Check which wording in the issue
supports the decision before applying

28
00:01:37,181 --> 00:01:37,421
it.

29
00:01:39,695 --> 00:01:42,379
Think of the materials for a training
lesson.

30
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:46,675
A screenshot can go into a vision-
capable model for a description.

31
00:01:46,756 --> 00:01:50,063
A written brief can go into an image
model for an illustration.

32
00:01:50,726 --> 00:01:54,109
Audio can go into a transcription system
for text.

33
00:01:54,162 --> 00:01:57,894
Check a screenshot description against
the visible controls.

34
00:01:57,975 --> 00:02:01,457
Check a transcript against an important
sentence in the recording.

35
00:02:03,471 --> 00:02:05,834
Some tools combine these steps.

36
00:02:05,915 --> 00:02:10,855
A question about zero quantities might
first retrieve the most relevant note.

37
00:02:10,908 --> 00:02:14,088
The language model then uses that note
to draft an answer.

38
00:02:14,746 --> 00:02:19,015
When you see the final paragraph, trace
it back through the citation.

39
00:02:19,096 --> 00:02:23,848
If retrieval found an outdated rule, the
answer can sound convincing and still

40
00:02:23,902 --> 00:02:25,184
use the wrong evidence.

41
00:02:27,089 --> 00:02:31,116
Choose a task from the training
repository and finish this sentence.

42
00:02:31,197 --> 00:02:35,197
“I need this input turned into this
output, and I will check it by doing

43
00:02:35,251 --> 00:02:35,625
this.”

44
00:02:36,295 --> 00:02:41,154
For example, use a language model to
explain the validator and verify the

45
00:02:41,208 --> 00:02:43,701
explanation against the code and a test.

46
00:02:44,405 --> 00:02:47,787
Once that check is clear, you're ready
to compare options.
